Overlay patch 0021 (rev4 = reference 0019 rev4, SYNC WAVE 2): harden
Metal UploadTexture — clamp + null-guard + mip-aware byte ledger +
DEFERRED mip-chain generation.
rev1: clamp dims to GetMaxTextureSize (Metal aborts past the limit; the
OOB-load fallback makes degenerate 16384x1+ strips). Keep SOURCE stride.
rev2: null-guard newTexture (nil under memory pressure was dereferenced);
exact GPU byte ledger feeding the 0022 budget eviction.
rev3: >=256 px textures get a full mip chain — single-level minification
of 2048px+ pack textures cost the Ship of Harkinian port 31 ms GPU frames in vistas
(3.8 ms with mips).
rev4 (NON-OPTIONAL freeze fix): mip generation must NOT create per-upload
mid-frame command buffers — the frame's CB is enqueue()d and uncommitted,
so upload CBs stack behind it and >63 uploads/frame deadlock
commandBuffer() (reference: Hyrule Field frozen with music playing).
Uploads push retained textures onto a pending list; EndFrame encodes ONE
generateMipmaps blit pass on the frame's own command buffer. Mips valid
next frame; ledger counts +1/3 for chains.
rev12 (VR R10 item 2): `gSohVRDiagNoMips` — the ONE engine-side row of
the in-headset device bisect kit, "Texture mipmaps". A texture uploaded
this frame has UNINITIALISED mip levels until the deferred blit above,
and sampling those minified is arbitrary memory read as colour — a
textbook rainbow. rev11 mitigates it with a LOD0 sampler; whether that
mitigation is complete on a DEVICE driver that does not zero-fill fresh
allocations is exactly the question the kit exists to answer without a
rebuild. Raised, the flag (a) stops new uploads being mipmapped at all
and (b) puts every bound texture on the rev11 LOD0 sampler immediately,
so the toggle changes the picture on the NEXT frame rather than waiting
for the texture cache to churn. 0 in every shipping frame, and on every
target but visionOS it is never written at all.

